From e082f7e478ebd618255bd5847c810e08c7c50044 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Reid W <westwood.reid@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 09:09:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] plant-idiom: state the two self-binding replacement forms and
 their meanings

The section said "write the binding so the two names differ" and offered two
renaming spellings. Both are correct, but they mean the same thing, so the
guidance never told an author which form to reach for or why a model with
repeated subsystems would be refused.

State the actual rule. The two replacement forms are owner-qualified
(`'Probe Plant'::R`) and feature chain (`plant_a.R`), and they differ in
which feature the reference lands on -- definition-level versus
concrete-occurrence. That difference decides whether the model elaborates
once a part def has more than one occurrence: the qualified form is refused
with SI_OCCURRENCE_AMBIGUOUS, the chain names which occurrence. Bare
renaming is kept as the smallest edit when the consumer sits inside the def.

Anchored two ways. Spec: KerML 7.4.6.2 states the qualified-name versus
feature-chain distinction directly, and SysML v2 Part 1 7.17.2 uses the
qualified form to fix this same collision in its own normative example.
Measured: all four forms run through the shipped exact route 2026-08-15
against a common probe model, results tabulated in the doc -- in every case
that elaborates, both consumers wire to ONE entry point carrying the
occurrence's override.

Also record why `:>>` does not work here, since it is the intuitive guess:
after `redefines` the name resolves through the owning type's supertypes with
the owner's own namespace excluded (KerML 8.2.3.5.1), so a calc usage cannot
reach the enclosing part's attribute, and the redefined feature must be one
otherwise inherited (KerML 7.3.4.5). Its proper job here is the occurrence
override the idiom already uses.

One rationale is explicitly ruled out: redefinition does NOT fan a modelled
value into per-consumer copies. KerML makes the redefining feature's values
be the redefined feature's values over its domain, so that argument is not
citable and is not used.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
