-Write the binding so the two names differ. Two forms are in the accepted corpus:
-
-- suffix the parameter — `in radius_in = radius`, as `fusion_tea` does throughout;
-- give the attribute the qualifying name — `in length = plant_length`, as `wi014_toy` does.
-
-For a value that lives on another part, name the path: `in driver_cost = driver.cost`.
+### What to write instead
+
+Two forms replace a self-binding, and **they do not mean the same thing.** The difference is
+which feature the reference lands on, and it decides whether the model elaborates at all once
+a part def has more than one occurrence.
+
+**Form 1 — owner-qualified: `in R = 'Probe Plant'::R`.** "The attribute as the *definition*
+declares it." The reference resolves statically to the def-level feature, so the occurrence has
+to be recovered from the consumer's context. **Only usable when exactly one occurrence of that
+part def is in the consumer's context.** With two, elaboration refuses:
+`SI_OCCURRENCE_AMBIGUOUS: consumer context contains 2 leaf occurrences`. It never guesses.
+
+**Form 2 — feature chain: `in R = plant_a.R`.** "The attribute on *this* occurrence." The path
+is part of the referent's identity, so the reference lands on the named occurrence and picks up
+whatever `:>>` override that occurrence carries. **This is the only form that can distinguish
+two occurrences of one part def**, and the only one that scales to a plant with repeated
+subsystems.
+
+KerML states the distinction directly (§7.4.6.2): "while the qualified name `axle::halfAxles`
+statically resolves to `Axle::halfAxles`, in the Feature chain `axle.halfAxles`, `halfAxles` is
+understood to be specifically the feature **as nested in** `axle`." SysML v2 Part 1 §7.17.2 uses
+the qualified form to fix this exact same-name collision in its own normative example.
+
+**A third option, when the collision is all you are fixing.** If the consumer sits *inside* the
+part def, just make the names differ and bind bare — `in radius_in = radius`, as `fusion_tea`
+does throughout, or `in length = plant_length`, as `wi014_toy` does. A calc nested in the def
+gets one instance per occurrence, so this scales to repeated subsystems without a chain. This is
+the smallest edit and the right default for in-place migration of a self-binding.
+
+**What does not work: `:>>` (redefinition).** Reassigning the parameter cannot reach the
+enclosing part's attribute. After `redefines`, the name is resolved through the *supertypes* of
+the owning type, with the owner's own namespace deliberately excluded (KerML §8.2.3.5.1), so
+inside a calc usage the search space is the calc definition — the enclosing part is unreachable.
+The redefined feature must also be one "that would otherwise be inherited from supertypes of its
+owning type" (KerML §7.3.4.5), which a sibling part attribute is not. Redefinition's proper job
+here is the one the plant idiom already uses: overriding an inherited *default* at an occurrence,
+`:>> R = 12.7`. (Writing it fully qualified as `:>> 'Probe Plant'::R` lands in genuine spec
+tension — the Subsetting prose requires the domains to conform and they do not, while the
+machine-checkable OCL only requires accessibility and would pass. Do not rely on it.)
+
+**Verified, not inferred.** All four forms were run through the shipped exact route on
+2026-08-15 against a common probe model (one attribute `R : Real default 3.0`, a concrete
+occurrence overriding `:>> R = 12.7`, two calc consumers):
+
+| written form | consumer sits | 2+ occurrences | outcome |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `in R = R` | anywhere | — | refused, `SI_SELF_BINDING` |
+| `in r_in = R` | inside the part def | yes | one entry point per occurrence, override honored |
+| `in R = 'Probe Plant'::R` | outside the def | no | one entry point at the occurrence |
+| `in R = 'Probe Plant'::R` | outside the def | **yes** | refused, `SI_OCCURRENCE_AMBIGUOUS` |
+| `in R = plant_a.R` | outside the def | yes | one entry point per named occurrence |
+
+In every case that elaborates, both consumers wire to **one** entry point carrying the
+occurrence's `12.7` — not the definition's `3.0` default, and not a copy per consumer.
+
+One rationale to avoid, because it is not true: redefinition does **not** fan a modelled value
+out into per-consumer copies. KerML makes the redefining feature's values *be* the redefined
+feature's values over its domain. The reasons to avoid `:>>` here are the resolution and
+inheritance rules above, not copy semantics.
 
